WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.645 --> 00:00:03.946
- Don't let people's outside perspectives

00:00:03.946 --> 00:00:05.221
change what you do.

00:00:05.221 --> 00:00:09.623
The easiest thing to do
is to not live presently.

00:00:09.623 --> 00:00:11.985
I don't want to be in the
studio every single day,

00:00:11.985 --> 00:00:12.933
all the time.

00:00:12.933 --> 00:00:14.560
You know, we never set out to be like,

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:17.887
yo we're going to make this
song about thrift shops,

00:00:17.887 --> 00:00:20.977
and it's going to go multi-platinum.

00:00:20.977 --> 00:00:22.987
Cause I know that I want to live up to

00:00:22.987 --> 00:00:24.201
my fullest potential.

00:00:24.201 --> 00:00:26.743
I want to be the best human
being that I can possibly be.

00:00:26.743 --> 00:00:28.702
I had always wanted to get sober.

00:00:28.702 --> 00:00:30.400
I could never get sober.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.048
Did you ever want to just
give up on your dream?

00:00:34.048 --> 00:00:36.259
Yes, earlier today.

00:00:36.259 --> 00:00:37.660
It's not about what you're building.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.838
It's not about the talent
that you're going with.

00:00:39.838 --> 00:00:42.210
It's about the hours that
you put into that talent.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:44.146
We did poetry readings.

00:00:44.146 --> 00:00:46.393
Really, what that was
was just acapella raps.

00:00:46.393 --> 00:00:48.695
And, I don't want to
look back and be like,

00:00:48.695 --> 00:00:51.934
you had it all, and you
weren't even present for it.

00:00:51.934 --> 00:00:54.455
- He's an American rapper
from Seattle, Washington.

00:00:54.455 --> 00:00:57.015
Since 2000, he has independently released

00:00:57.015 --> 00:00:59.680
one mix tape, three EP's, and four albums.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:01.618
His single, Thrift Shop, hit number one

00:01:01.618 --> 00:01:05.208
on the US Billboard Hot 100 in 2013.

00:01:05.208 --> 00:01:07.181
He's Macklemore, and here are his top 10

00:01:07.181 --> 00:01:08.681
rules for success.

00:01:11.672 --> 00:01:15.160
- Don't let people's outside perspectives

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:17.605
change what you do, change who you are,

00:01:17.605 --> 00:01:21.524
dictate your happiness, dictate
your level of self-worth,

00:01:21.524 --> 00:01:23.151
affect your self-esteem.

00:01:23.151 --> 00:01:26.307
As an artist, that's
easier said than done.

00:01:26.307 --> 00:01:29.348
The public's perception does weigh on you.

00:01:29.348 --> 00:01:31.623
The box that they put
you in does affect you.

00:01:31.623 --> 00:01:33.526
I think that it's a constant fight

00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:36.756
to remember why you make
music, who you do it for,

00:01:36.756 --> 00:01:40.774
why it's important, and
to follow your process,

00:01:40.774 --> 00:01:43.470
and to not let anybody's outside opinion

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:45.137
dictate who you are.

00:01:45.998 --> 00:01:47.213
You know it.

00:01:47.213 --> 00:01:50.213
I think that the easiest thing to do

00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:53.458
is to not live presently.

00:01:53.458 --> 00:01:56.875
In life, we are consistently in the past,

00:01:58.235 --> 00:02:01.402
in the future, anywhere but right now.

00:02:02.714 --> 00:02:05.547
I think for myself as a performer,

00:02:06.976 --> 00:02:11.711
it is important to slow down,
and remember what I'm doing,

00:02:11.711 --> 00:02:15.381
why I'm here, remember
the words that I wrote,

00:02:15.381 --> 00:02:18.237
the feeling that I had when I wrote them,

00:02:18.237 --> 00:02:21.913
and deliver that in the
same emotion that I had

00:02:21.913 --> 00:02:24.448
when I was in that
moment writing the music.

00:02:24.448 --> 00:02:28.031
Both Ryan and I are,
at times, I don't want

00:02:29.105 --> 00:02:32.464
to say envious, but we
look at other people.

00:02:32.464 --> 00:02:34.065
Like, you take Kendrick.

00:02:34.065 --> 00:02:35.659
Kendrick is a rapper.

00:02:35.659 --> 00:02:37.388
Kendrick is in the studio right now.

00:02:37.388 --> 00:02:38.638
I guarantee it.

00:02:39.933 --> 00:02:43.681
He's not thinking about
that the merch design

00:02:43.681 --> 00:02:46.016
that we have right now
isn't fitting on a tank top

00:02:46.016 --> 00:02:47.933
the way that it should.

00:02:49.133 --> 00:02:50.133
- It's true.

00:02:52.527 --> 00:02:55.179
- It's a blessing and a curse.

00:02:55.179 --> 00:02:58.929
You know, we both, we
are the output in terms

00:02:59.791 --> 00:03:01.145
of anything creative.

00:03:01.145 --> 00:03:02.433
If it's a music video, if it's directing,

00:03:02.433 --> 00:03:06.171
if it's writing a treatment, if it's being

00:03:06.171 --> 00:03:07.825
a character in the music video,

00:03:07.825 --> 00:03:09.575
we are everything.

00:03:09.575 --> 00:03:10.867
Same thing with their merch, same thing

00:03:10.867 --> 00:03:13.419
with just the ideas
that we have full circle

00:03:13.419 --> 00:03:14.745
in terms of running a business.

00:03:14.745 --> 00:03:16.589
So, that's taking away time that we could

00:03:16.589 --> 00:03:20.621
be working on our specific
crafts, producing and rapping.

00:03:20.621 --> 00:03:23.446
So, I think that it's a balance.

00:03:23.446 --> 00:03:25.363
We both enjoy business.

00:03:26.391 --> 00:03:30.421
We both enjoy being holistically creative.

00:03:30.421 --> 00:03:31.441
That's a fulfilling thing.

00:03:31.441 --> 00:03:32.944
I don't want to be in the studio

00:03:32.944 --> 00:03:34.996
every single day, all the time.

00:03:34.996 --> 00:03:36.658
I want to be picking fonts.

00:03:36.658 --> 00:03:40.490
I want to be with Ryan,
color editing a music video.

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:42.417
That's something that we both enjoy.

00:03:42.417 --> 00:03:44.333
So, it's a balance, and I think that we

00:03:44.333 --> 00:03:46.561
wear enough hats that we can bounce around

00:03:46.561 --> 00:03:47.810
and be inspired.

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:49.382
It comes at a sacrifice.

00:03:49.382 --> 00:03:51.212
You know, we never set out to be like,

00:03:51.212 --> 00:03:54.642
yo we're going to make this
song about thrift shops,

00:03:54.642 --> 00:03:57.669
and it's going to go multi-platinum,

00:03:57.669 --> 00:04:00.763
and it's going to, we
didn't have, in our minds,

00:04:00.763 --> 00:04:02.593
any songs that would ever work at radio.

00:04:02.593 --> 00:04:05.374
So, I think we went into the
same process with this one.

00:04:05.374 --> 00:04:07.990
Maybe nothing ends up on the radio.

00:04:07.990 --> 00:04:09.066
That's great.

00:04:09.066 --> 00:04:11.025
- (beep) money.

00:04:11.025 --> 00:04:12.041
(all laugh)

00:04:12.041 --> 00:04:13.856
- That's because we have (beep) money.

00:04:13.856 --> 00:04:14.689
- There you go.

00:04:14.689 --> 00:04:15.774
There you go.

00:04:15.774 --> 00:04:19.152
- No, it's truly because
the art is what matters,

00:04:19.152 --> 00:04:21.902
and not sales, and not the radio.

00:04:22.819 --> 00:04:24.223
We care about the art.

00:04:24.223 --> 00:04:26.713
If that's the compromise
of not tapping back

00:04:26.713 --> 00:04:30.328
into a pop audience,
or not being in arenas,

00:04:30.328 --> 00:04:31.979
or not being in theaters, or whatever,

00:04:31.979 --> 00:04:33.738
we're going to make the
art that we want to make.

00:04:33.738 --> 00:04:38.177
As a songwriter, you
strive for that perfection.

00:04:38.177 --> 00:04:40.993
The thing that can kill you with it,

00:04:40.993 --> 00:04:44.052
and that I learned from
that website that I went to,

00:04:44.052 --> 00:04:46.145
the thing that can kill you with it

00:04:46.145 --> 00:04:49.213
is if you let that perfectionism

00:04:49.213 --> 00:04:52.931
stop you from beginning
in the first place.

00:04:52.931 --> 00:04:56.164
So much of what goes into making a song,

00:04:56.164 --> 00:04:58.818
it takes courage, it
takes battling some fear.

00:04:58.818 --> 00:05:00.313
If you have a good song, you've got

00:05:00.313 --> 00:05:01.773
to write another good song.

00:05:01.773 --> 00:05:03.892
There's expectation.

00:05:03.892 --> 00:05:06.897
The pressure, first and
foremost, comes from yourself,

00:05:06.897 --> 00:05:09.424
so staring in front of
a blank piece of paper

00:05:09.424 --> 00:05:11.839
is a challenging thing,
and it always has been.

00:05:11.839 --> 00:05:13.974
It was challenging when
I was 16 years old,

00:05:13.974 --> 00:05:15.065
writing for the first time.

00:05:15.065 --> 00:05:18.685
It was challenging at 30 years old.

00:05:18.685 --> 00:05:19.518
It's hard.

00:05:20.696 --> 00:05:23.964
I know that I want to live
up to my fullest potential.

00:05:23.964 --> 00:05:26.883
I want to be the best human
being that I can possible be.

00:05:26.883 --> 00:05:29.395
I want to make the best art that I can.

00:05:29.395 --> 00:05:32.978
Sometimes, you have to
write just to write.

00:05:33.958 --> 00:05:36.365
If that turns into something great,

00:05:36.365 --> 00:05:38.986
hone it until it's the
best that it can be,

00:05:38.986 --> 00:05:42.713
and then eventually put
the paintbrush down.

00:05:42.713 --> 00:05:45.156
Put the final coat of
lacquer on the canvas,

00:05:45.156 --> 00:05:46.087
and just walk away, and that's perfect.

00:05:46.087 --> 00:05:47.504
That's Same Love.

00:05:48.666 --> 00:05:51.083
- In 2009, when the mix tape.

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:55.908
There was a period where,
I think around that time,

00:05:55.908 --> 00:05:58.272
you were kind of like, "Is this something

00:05:58.272 --> 00:06:01.519
"that's going to work for me or not?"

00:06:01.519 --> 00:06:02.965
What is it that got you through?

00:06:02.965 --> 00:06:05.298
How'd you work through that?

00:06:08.136 --> 00:06:11.011
- Yeah, it's tough to
say exactly what it was.

00:06:11.011 --> 00:06:12.660
I think it was hard work.

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:13.772
It was discipline.

00:06:13.772 --> 00:06:16.514
It was knowing that if
this doesn't work out,

00:06:16.514 --> 00:06:19.804
then I will have to get a nine-to-five.

00:06:19.804 --> 00:06:21.340
I don't want to do that.

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:23.923
I really don't want to do that.

00:06:25.014 --> 00:06:27.406
It was working with Ryan,

00:06:27.406 --> 00:06:30.573
and putting everything into the music.

00:06:31.717 --> 00:06:34.283
Leading up to that point, I think that

00:06:34.283 --> 00:06:36.519
you have to look at my track record.

00:06:36.519 --> 00:06:38.662
I had always wanted to get sober.

00:06:38.662 --> 00:06:40.690
I could never get sober.

00:06:40.690 --> 00:06:43.288
I couldn't make music while
I was under the influence

00:06:43.288 --> 00:06:44.521
of drugs or alcohol.

00:06:44.521 --> 00:06:46.720
I finally got sober in
2008, got out of rehab,

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:48.245
and was like, alright, this is it.

00:06:48.245 --> 00:06:50.944
I've wasted years of my life.

00:06:50.944 --> 00:06:54.744
If this is going to
work, it has to work now.

00:06:54.744 --> 00:06:56.833
There's no alternative.

00:06:56.833 --> 00:07:01.000
I met somebody that had
the same dedication as me,

00:07:02.223 --> 00:07:04.338
that put everything into the music,

00:07:04.338 --> 00:07:07.450
everything into the
craft, and didn't go out,

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:10.117
didn't really have many friends.

00:07:14.275 --> 00:07:16.179
Just kidding, just kidding.

00:07:16.179 --> 00:07:18.439
We only hang out with each other.

00:07:18.439 --> 00:07:21.531
No, it's somebody that
wasn't going to be like,

00:07:21.531 --> 00:07:23.480
up until that point, I was making music

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:26.281
with people that were constantly
putting their priorities

00:07:26.281 --> 00:07:27.192
into getting--

00:07:27.192 --> 00:07:28.025
- Cooler people.

00:07:28.025 --> 00:07:29.252
- No, not at all.

00:07:29.252 --> 00:07:32.764
Getting loaded, or chasing
women, or whatever,

00:07:32.764 --> 00:07:34.809
and that's what I did for a long time.

00:07:34.809 --> 00:07:36.921
It lead me to being broke and in rehab,

00:07:36.921 --> 00:07:39.088
and moving back into my parents' house,

00:07:39.088 --> 00:07:40.505
which is awesome.

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:44.059
I wanted a career, and Ryan was somebody

00:07:44.059 --> 00:07:46.212
that had the same discipline,

00:07:46.212 --> 00:07:47.984
and sacrificed everything.

00:07:47.984 --> 00:07:51.654
Did you ever want to just
give up on your dream?

00:07:51.654 --> 00:07:53.237
Yes, earlier today.

00:07:54.385 --> 00:07:56.847
It happens all the time.

00:07:56.847 --> 00:07:58.880
That's part of being
an artist, I feel like,

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:01.493
is being challenged to
push through those moments

00:08:01.493 --> 00:08:03.932
of is this what I'm supposed to do.

00:08:03.932 --> 00:08:05.881
The people that succeed,
and the people that make it

00:08:05.881 --> 00:08:09.214
in the music industry, I
think that never goes away.

00:08:09.214 --> 00:08:11.476
That's something that
you're always fighting

00:08:11.476 --> 00:08:13.143
and working against.

00:08:14.159 --> 00:08:16.331
That's the process of being an artist.

00:08:16.331 --> 00:08:19.011
By the time I got to college,

00:08:19.011 --> 00:08:21.605
I made this album called
The Language of My World.

00:08:21.605 --> 00:08:25.175
(audience cheers)

00:08:25.175 --> 00:08:28.385
And, with this album,
I did the same thing.

00:08:28.385 --> 00:08:31.260
I locked myself in my room
for like, all of college.

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:32.285
That was it.

00:08:32.285 --> 00:08:35.281
I just worked my ass off in my room,

00:08:35.281 --> 00:08:37.401
and kept writing, kept recording.

00:08:37.401 --> 00:08:39.055
I wasn't great going into college,

00:08:39.055 --> 00:08:40.971
but by the time I got out, I had kind of

00:08:40.971 --> 00:08:43.638
learned a little bit of a craft.

00:08:46.082 --> 00:08:48.371
By the time that I got out of college,

00:08:48.371 --> 00:08:52.538
I read this book called
Outliers, by Malcolm Gladwell.

00:08:54.402 --> 00:08:55.363
Nobody's read it.

00:08:55.363 --> 00:08:56.974
(audience cheers)

00:08:56.974 --> 00:08:57.891
One person.

00:08:58.782 --> 00:09:02.615
Anyway, this book examines
this theory, right.

00:09:04.970 --> 00:09:06.454
Malcolm Gladwell makes this point.

00:09:06.454 --> 00:09:08.294
He says it's not about
what you're building.

00:09:08.294 --> 00:09:10.316
It's not about the talent
that you're going with.

00:09:10.316 --> 00:09:14.229
It's about the hours that
you put into that talent.

00:09:14.229 --> 00:09:16.041
He says that if you want to be a master

00:09:16.041 --> 00:09:17.906
at whatever it is that you love to do,

00:09:17.906 --> 00:09:19.526
if you want to develop that craft to its

00:09:19.526 --> 00:09:22.320
utmost potential, what
you have to do is work.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:23.756
You have to work a lot.

00:09:23.756 --> 00:09:24.938
You have to put in hours.

00:09:24.938 --> 00:09:28.807
He says that if you put
in around 10,000 hours

00:09:28.807 --> 00:09:31.523
into that one thing, that eventually you

00:09:31.523 --> 00:09:33.440
will master that craft.

00:09:35.746 --> 00:09:37.770
I don't know how close
I am to 10,000 hours,

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:40.556
but every single time we step on stage,

00:09:40.556 --> 00:09:42.473
we get one step closer.

00:09:43.452 --> 00:09:46.595
Hip-hop in Seattle in the late '90's

00:09:46.595 --> 00:09:48.012
was grunge music.

00:09:50.646 --> 00:09:52.479
It was very different.

00:09:53.582 --> 00:09:54.584
It was a very small scene.

00:09:54.584 --> 00:09:58.494
It was a couple hundred people verses,

00:09:58.494 --> 00:10:00.494
it's very different now.

00:10:01.483 --> 00:10:05.381
We would, we didn't
have a place to perform.

00:10:05.381 --> 00:10:06.356
There was no venues.

00:10:06.356 --> 00:10:07.535
There was no people to fill the venues

00:10:07.535 --> 00:10:09.297
if we could book the venues.

00:10:09.297 --> 00:10:13.068
So, what we did was,
we did poetry readings.

00:10:13.068 --> 00:10:16.735
Really what that was
was just acapella raps.

00:10:17.679 --> 00:10:20.358
Those took place on Mondays.

00:10:20.358 --> 00:10:21.870
A lot of people would get together.

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:25.926
A lot of people being,
like, 15, 20 people,

00:10:25.926 --> 00:10:27.318
and spit each other raps that they'd

00:10:27.318 --> 00:10:28.390
been working on that week.

00:10:28.390 --> 00:10:31.074
That was a place that we
honed our skills on a stage.

00:10:31.074 --> 00:10:33.618
I don't know if anybody
out there's a performer,

00:10:33.618 --> 00:10:37.289
but it's a lot more challenging to perform

00:10:37.289 --> 00:10:40.183
in front of 15 people acapella

00:10:40.183 --> 00:10:43.191
than it is for 20,000 people.

00:10:43.191 --> 00:10:45.274
It's a lot more intimate.

00:10:46.329 --> 00:10:48.497
That got me to a place of performance

00:10:48.497 --> 00:10:50.437
where I felt comfortable,

00:10:50.437 --> 00:10:52.529
and was able to then
transition that to the stage.

00:10:52.529 --> 00:10:55.034
It was an imperative part of my process.

00:10:55.034 --> 00:10:58.591
Be grateful, be happy, and enjoy this

00:10:58.591 --> 00:11:00.545
because it's not promised.

00:11:00.545 --> 00:11:02.321
Who knows where we're
going to be in a year,

00:11:02.321 --> 00:11:03.746
two years, three years?

00:11:03.746 --> 00:11:06.132
We're at this position now.

00:11:06.132 --> 00:11:07.626
I don't want to look back and be like,

00:11:07.626 --> 00:11:11.173
you had it all, and you
weren't even present for it.

00:11:11.173 --> 00:11:12.533
You weren't able to enjoy it.

00:11:12.533 --> 00:11:16.728
I want to be here, be
now, and be grateful.

00:11:16.728 --> 00:11:17.944
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:11:17.944 --> 00:11:20.542
I made this video because
Anna Jung asked me to.

00:11:20.542 --> 00:11:21.975
So, if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:11:21.975 --> 00:11:23.606
that you want me to profile next,

00:11:23.606 --> 00:11:24.970
leave it in the comments below,

00:11:24.970 --> 00:11:25.965
and I'll see what I can do.

00:11:25.965 --> 00:11:27.415
I'd also love to know
which of Macklemore's

00:11:27.415 --> 00:11:29.744
top 10 rules had the
biggest impact on you.

00:11:29.744 --> 00:11:31.277
Which one did you like the most?

00:11:31.277 --> 00:11:33.326
Leave it in the comments,
and I'll join the discussion.

00:11:33.326 --> 00:11:34.703
Thank you so much for watching.

00:11:34.703 --> 00:11:38.286
Continue to believe,
and I'll see you soon.

